Reading the code, I think the optimum are piles of >=3D5 objects, only
the lowermost of which is a cursed horn or ivory ring. (Lowermost
could also mean uppermost, I didn't check.) This yields expected one
skeleton per 32 bony things actually polymorphed. Recuperated
instrument horns and ivory rings count as extra polymorphs, resisting
items do not.

The code walkthrough starts with zap.c, line 2744. bhitpile() only
calls create_polymon() once, so you can get at most one golem from
each pile. Independently for each object in the pile, the chance of a
golem/skeleton is 95% (resists completely, zap.c#line1506) * 100%
(obj_shudders()) * 1/(45+Luck) (golem, zap.c#line1163). If several
objects give rise to a golem, the material from the last one
determines its type. That means beyond the first one, any horn objects
are wasted, and non-horn objects even harmful.


